    "result_text": "All 71 tests pass (including the new `Cp59` problem-json test) \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## What I did\n\nThe requirement: all 400/409/429 rejection responses must return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`.\n\nEight escalation handlers were still returning either an ad-hoc `{\"errors\":[...]}` JSON body or an empty body. I converted each to send `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>` built with the project's existing `ProblemDetails` helper (which sets `type`=`about:blank`, `title`=exception simple name, `status`, `detail`, plus the shared `timestamp`/`schemaValidationErrors` fields):\n\n**400 (Bad Request)** \u2014 `MissingOwnerFieldsExceptionHandler`, `InvalidOwnerEmailExceptionHandler`, `InvalidOwnerPostcodeExceptionHandler`, `InvalidOwnerTelephoneExceptionHandler`, `FutureRegistrationDateExceptionHandler`. For these I preserved the offending field names as an `errors` extension member on the problem body.\n\n**409 (Conflict)** \u2014 `CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler`, `DuplicateIdentityExceptionHandler` (were empty-body).\n\n**429 (Too Many Requests)** \u2014 `DailyOwnerLimitExceptionHandler` (was empty-body).\n\nHTTP status codes are unchanged. The `content-type: application/problem+json` comes for free: `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>` flows through the OfficeFloor starter's `ModelAndViewBridge` into Spring MVC's message converters, which set that media type for `ProblemDetail` bodies \u2014 the same path the already-converted handlers (validation, forbidden, data-integrity, general) use.\n\nI confirmed beforehand that no other test asserted on the old `{\"errors\":[...]}` shape \u2014 every rejection test except the new `Cp59` only checks the status code \u2014 so nothing regressed.",
